### 7. Existing examples of specific / business-rule status codes
Yes — the create endpoint already rejects with business-rule statuses via `throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.X, "reason")`, all in `OwnerRestControllerV1.addOwner` and its helpers:

- `HttpStatus.CONFLICT` (409) — telephone already in use (lines 116-119), duplicate household (121-125), city capacity reached (126-129).
- `HttpStatus.BAD_REQUEST` (400) — telephone not normalizable (`normalizeTelephone`, 329-332), invalid email (`normalizeEmail`, 372-375), blank address (`normalizeAddress`, 402-405).

There is **no** existing 429 / `TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` usage in the main code (only referenced in the acceptance test as `status().isTooManyRequests()`). Your edit should add, in `addOwner` (alongside the other pre-save checks, before `saveOwner`), a count of owners whose `registrationDate` equals `LocalDate.now()` and, when it is `>= 100`, `throw new ResponseStatusException(HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS, "...")`. `HttpStatus.TOO_MANY_REQUESTS` (429) is already available from the `org.springframework.http.HttpStatus` import present in the controller, and the existing `ResponseStatusException` advice handler will surface it as a real 429.
